  "settingsHelp": "Help",
  "tutorialSectionTitle": "Guided tours",
  "tutorialSectionNote": "Ten tours that cover the whole app, one subject each. Follow them all and nothing here will be a mystery. You can leave any of them at any time.",
  "tutorialOfferTitle": "Shall I show you around?",
  "tutorialOfferBody": "A ten-step tour of what everything is and where it lives. There are nine more, one per subject, in Settings under Help. You can leave whenever you like.",
  "tutorialOfferAccept": "Start",
  "tutorialOfferDecline": "Not now",
  "tutorialProgress": "{position} of {total}",
  "@tutorialProgress": {"placeholders": {"position": {"type": "int"}, "total": {"type": "int"}}},
  "tutorialSkip": "Leave",
  "tutorialBack": "Back",
  "tutorialNext": "Next",
  "tutorialDone": "Done",
  "tutorialWelcomeTitle": "Welcome to FeRN",
  "tutorialWelcomeBody": "FeRN keeps the media you collect: it brings it in, sorts it with tags and creators, and can learn to recognise what appears in it. Use Next or the arrow keys, and Escape to leave.",
  "tutorialSidebarTitle": "This is how you get around",
  "tutorialSidebarBody": "Every screen is here: your library, what you bring in, favourites, the bin, and the managers for creators, tags, fernies and models. The button at the top folds it away when you need the room.",
  "tutorialImportTitle": "Content comes in here",
  "tutorialImportBody": "You bring content from a remote source — Reddit, Pixiv, Danbooru, Gelbooru, Pinterest, Pawchive — or from a folder on your disk. Whatever arrives waits here until you accept it, so nothing lands in your library without you seeing it.",
  "tutorialContentTitle": "And this is where it all shows up",
  "tutorialContentBody": "Your library: everything you have accepted. A click opens the viewer, right-click brings up the actions, and you can tick several to work on them together.",
  "tutorialTagsTitle": "Tag by dragging",
  "tutorialTagsBody": "The tags in the menu are also places to drop: drag one or several items onto one and they come out tagged. Clicking a tag filters the library down to what carries it.",
  "tutorialCreateTitle": "Creating things",
  "tutorialCreateBody": "This is where creators, tags, fernies and models are born. A creator is who made something; a tag is anything you want to group by; a fernie is a face or a thing you want FeRN to learn.",
  "tutorialSearchTitle": "Searching",
  "tutorialSearchBody": "Type and FeRN offers what matches: a tag, a creator, a description. What you pick becomes a chip, and chips stack — two of them show what meets both.",
  "tutorialSettingsTitle": "And everything else is here",
  "tutorialSettingsBody": "Language, theme, folders, remote sources, recognition and the content lock. Under Help you will find the other nine tours: one per subject, and between them they cover the whole app.",

  "tourGeneralTitle": "The general tour",
  "tourGeneralDescription": "What FeRN is, what each screen is for and where everything lives. Start here.",
  "tourImportingTitle": "Bringing content in",
  "tourImportingDescription": "Where content comes from, how you review it and what it takes for it to reach your library.",
  "tourImporting1Title": "Where from, and how much",
  "tourImporting1Body": "Pick a source — a remote one or a folder on this computer — say whether you want everything or only what is new since last time, and press Bring. A remote source brings what you have favourited there.",
  "tourImporting2Title": "Each source needs its keys",
  "tourImporting2Body": "Remote sources need your account details, and you put them in Settings under Remote sources. Each one explains how to get them: they are not your password but keys the platform gives out for this.",
  "tourImporting3Title": "Nothing here is yours yet",
  "tourImporting3Body": "This grid is the inbox: what has been brought in, waiting for you to say what to do with it. It is already on your disk, but it is not in your library and it does not show up in searches.",
  "tourImporting4Title": "Open one and decide",
  "tourImporting4Body": "A click opens it in the viewer. There you save it — and it becomes final — or you discard it. Discarding takes it out of the database and asks whether to delete the file as well.",
  "tourImporting5Title": "The details, without leaving the viewer",
  "tourImporting5Body": "The side panel is where it gets a creator, tags, a description and links. You edit it while you look at it, which is when you actually know what it is. Saving also moves you on to the next one.",
  "tourManagersTitle": "Tags, people and creators",
  "tourManagersDescription": "Everything you use to sort your library: the tag tree, siblings, people, creators and automatic tagging.",
  "tourManagers1Title": "Creators",
  "tourManagers1Body": "A creator is whoever made something: an artist, an account, a studio. Each item has one and only one. Picking one here fills the screen with everything of theirs.",
  "tourManagers2Title": "Their card",
  "tourManagers2Body": "Name, avatar and the links to where they publish. Those links are not decoration: they are what lets FeRN put the creator on by itself when something arrives from one of those addresses.",
  "tourManagers3Title": "Avatars",
  "tourManagers3Body": "Click the picture and you choose where it comes from: a file on this computer, or your own library. From your library you can crop a square out of any image, which is what an avatar actually is.",
  "tourManagers4Title": "Tags work the same way",
  "tourManagers4Body": "With one difference: an item has one creator but as many tags as you like. A tag is anything you want to group by — a series, a colour, a mood, a place.",
  "tourManagers5Title": "Tags hang from tags",
  "tourManagers5Body": "Drag one onto another and it hangs from it. That is what makes tagging worth it: putting on a child tag puts on everything above it, so something tagged as a character also comes up under its series.",
  "tourFernieTitle": "Fernies and the marking mode",
  "tourFernieDescription": "What a fernie is, where its examples come from and every way of marking regions on your content.",
  "tourFernie1Title": "What a fernie is",
  "tourFernie1Body": "A face, a character or an object you want FeRN to learn to recognise in your content. It is not a tag: a tag says what something is, a fernie is an example of what something looks like.",
  "tourFernie2Title": "Link it to something",
  "tourFernie2Body": "Each fernie can point at a tag or a creator. That link is what turns recognition into tagging: when the fernie is found, that is what gets proposed. With nothing linked it only serves to train.",
  "tourFernie3Title": "Its regions",
  "tourFernie3Body": "Each crop is one example of it, and those examples are what a model learns from. The more and the more varied, the better: with little variety it learns the background instead of the thing.",
  "tourFernie4Title": "Marking them in the viewer",
  "tourFernie4Body": "Open something and enter fernie mode. With the marking tool, drag a box around what you want and pick whose it is. On video and GIFs the region belongs to the frame you are on.",
  "tourFernie5Title": "Fixing what you marked",
  "tourFernie5Body": "The other tool selects what is already there: move it, resize it by its handles, give it to another fernie or delete it. Nothing is written until you leave saving, so you can undo on the way.",
  "tourModelsTitle": "Models and recognition",
  "tourModels1Title": "Your models",
  "tourModels1Body": "A model is what actually recognises. You build it out of the fernies you give it, you train it once, and from then on it can look at your content and say what it sees.",
  "tourModels2Title": "First, the environment",
  "tourModels2Body": "Recognition runs on Python, and FeRN installs it for you: Settings, Recognition, and press install. It is a few hundred megabytes and it is done once. With a graphics card it goes much faster.",
  "tourModels3Title": "Building one",
  "tourModels3Body": "You choose its fernies and what it has to answer: whether each one is there or not, or which of them it found and where. The second needs at least two fernies and regions drawn on the content.",
  "tourModels4Title": "Training takes a while",
  "tourModels4Body": "Minutes to hours, depending on how much you gave it and what machine you have. It runs in the background and you can keep using FeRN; the indicator up top says which epoch it is on and roughly how long is left.",
  "tourModels5Title": "Recognising",
  "tourModels5Body": "A trained model goes over whatever content you point it at — one item, a selection, or the whole library — and proposes what it sees. Below its confidence threshold it proposes nothing.",
  "tourDuplicatesTitle": "Repeated content",
  "tourDuplicatesDescription": "How repeats are found, how you decide which copy stays and what makes two things count as the same.",
  "tourDuplicates1Title": "Finding repeats",
  "tourDuplicates1Body": "Press Search now and FeRN goes over your whole library working out a fingerprint of each item. The first time can take a while; after that it only fingerprints what is new.",
  "tourDuplicates2Title": "The groups",
  "tourDuplicates2Body": "Each group is copies that look alike enough to be the same thing — the same picture in two sizes, or saved twice from different places. Groups you have already answered do not come back.",
  "tourDuplicates3Title": "Choosing which one stays",
  "tourDuplicates3Body": "You pick the copy you keep and the rest are discarded. Before that you can merge into the one that stays the tags, the creator, the favourite mark and the description of the ones that go.",
  "tourDuplicates4Title": "Or say they are not the same",
  "tourDuplicates4Body": "If the group got it wrong, say so and it will not be offered again. Two things that merely look alike are not a repeat, and only you can tell the difference.",
  "tourDuplicates5Title": "The threshold, in Settings",
  "tourDuplicates5Body": "How different two items can be and still count as the same. Raising it groups more and starts lumping together things that merely look alike; lowering it only catches near-identical copies.",

  "tourModelsDescription": "The Python environment, how a model is built and trained, what it proposes, and how the tree decides which ones run.",
  "tourModels6Title": "Nothing is applied by itself",
  "tourModels6Body": "What it sees stays a suggestion until you accept it, in the item panel or in bulk from the import screen. Rejecting one means it will not be offered again for that item.",
  "tourModels7Title": "The model tree",
  "tourModels7Body": "A model that is not in the tree never runs when you recognise. The tree is what says which ones run and in what order.",
  "tourModels8Title": "Putting them in",
  "tourModels8Body": "The panel on the right holds the models that are out. Pick a node in the tree and what you add hangs from it. A model cannot hang from itself, and it cannot be in the tree twice.",
  "tourLibraryTitle": "The library and the viewer",
  "tourLibraryDescription": "The grid, selecting several at once, the right-click menu, and everything the viewer can do.",
  "tourSearchingTitle": "Searching and filtering",
  "tourSearchingDescription": "Chips that stack, free text, the filters in the bar and the other ways to narrow down what you see.",
  "tourNsfwTitle": "The content lock",
  "tourNsfwDescription": "A password that hides what you mark, what it hides along with it, and how it behaves when it is open and when it is closed.",
  "tourFilesTitle": "Files and upkeep",
  "tourFilesDescription": "Where your content lives on disk, what FeRN does with those files, and how to tidy up afterwards.",
  "tutorialViewerTitle": "The viewer",
  "tutorialViewerBody": "Opening something takes over the screen: zoom with the wheel, move between items with the arrows, and open the side panel to give it a creator, tags and a description while you are looking at it.",
  "tutorialJobsTitle": "What runs in the background",
  "tutorialJobsBody": "Imports, recognition, training and repeat scans all run behind you: this indicator says what is going on, how far along it is, and lets you stop it. You can keep using FeRN meanwhile.",
  "tourImporting6Title": "What you do not want again",
  "tourImporting6Body": "A remote source offers the same favourites every time. When you discard something you can also say do not offer this again: it will be skipped before it is even downloaded, and the list of what you blocked is in Settings under Database.",
  "tourImporting7Title": "Things that can happen on their own",
  "tourImporting7Body": "What arrives can be tagged on its own if the address it came from is linked to a tag, marked NSFW if you turn that on in the header, and passed through your models if you ask for that in Settings.",
  "tourImporting8Title": "Several at once",
  "tourImporting8Body": "You do not have to go one by one: tick several and the header lets you accept them all, discard them all, or accept every suggestion your models made above a confidence you choose.",
  "tourImporting9Title": "And then it is in Content",
  "tourImporting9Body": "What you saved leaves the import grid and appears in your library. From then on it is searchable, taggable and part of everything else FeRN does.",
  "tourLibrary1Title": "Everything you have",
  "tourLibrary1Body": "The grid keeps each item at its own proportions, so you recognise things by their shape as much as by their content. Scrolling loads more as you go.",
  "tourLibrary2Title": "Several at a time",
  "tourLibrary2Body": "Each cell has a tick in its corner: tick one and you are selecting. Shift and click stretches the selection to there, following the order on screen. What you do next applies to all of them.",
  "tourLibrary3Title": "The right-click menu",
  "tourLibrary3Body": "On any cell it opens what can be done: favourite it, send it to the bin, recognise it with your models, or open the folder it lives in. With a selection, it works on the whole selection.",
  "tourLibrary4Title": "The bin gives things back",
  "tourLibrary4Body": "Sending something to the bin does not delete it: it waits there for a while and you can put it back with everything it had. Emptying the bin is what deletes for good, and that one asks first.",
  "tourLibrary5Title": "Sorting and filtering here",
  "tourLibrary5Body": "The header says how many there are and lets you sort them — newest, oldest, at random — and keep only images, only video, or only what came from one source.",
  "tourLibrary6Title": "Inside the viewer",
  "tourLibrary6Body": "The wheel zooms, the middle button or space and drag moves you around, and a double click fits it back to the screen. The arrows go to the previous and the next one without leaving.",
  "tourLibrary7Title": "The information panel",
  "tourLibrary7Body": "Creator, tags, description and links, all editable on the spot. It also shows what your models suggest, and a button that explains where each tag on this item came from.",
  "tourLibrary8Title": "Favourites",
  "tourLibrary8Body": "The heart marks what you want to find again without having to remember how you tagged it. Everything marked lives on its own screen in the menu.",
  "tourLibrary9Title": "Video and animation",
  "tourLibrary9Body": "Video and GIFs play in the viewer with a timeline you can step through frame by frame. That matters later: a fernie region belongs to one frame, not to the whole clip.",
  "tourManagers6Title": "And some go together",
  "tourManagers6Body": "Dropping one onto another also offers to relate them instead: sisters. Sisters are not above or below each other — putting one on simply puts the other on too, because they always come as a pair.",
  "tourManagers7Title": "People are tags too",
  "tourManagers7Body": "A tag can be marked as a person: someone or a character that appears in the content. It is only a way of keeping the list tidy — the button at the top switches between the two lists — and everywhere else they behave like any other tag.",
  "tourManagers8Title": "Tagging by itself",
  "tourManagers8Body": "A tag can have addresses linked to it. Anything that arrives from one of those addresses comes in already tagged — and with everything above that tag, and its sisters. That is where most of your tagging will come from.",
  "tourManagers9Title": "A long list, tamed",
  "tourManagers9Body": "The chevron on a tag folds its branch away, and the search box above the list finds one by name. Both work here and in the side menu, and what you fold stays folded next time.",
  "tourManagers10Title": "Tagging in bulk",
  "tourManagers10Body": "From the library, drag one or several items onto a tag in the menu. It is the quick way, and it is why the menu tags are drop targets at all.",
  "tourManagers11Title": "Where did that tag come from?",
  "tourManagers11Body": "FeRN tags on its own through several routes, and in the panel they all look alike. The clock button on the item explains each one: you put it, an address matched, it was inherited, a model proposed it, a fernie brought it.",
  "tourSearching1Title": "The search bar",
  "tourSearching1Body": "It works from any screen. As you type, FeRN offers what matches: tags, creators and content by its description. It searches your library, not the internet.",
  "tourSearching2Title": "What you pick becomes a chip",
  "tourSearching2Body": "Picking a tag or a creator turns it into a chip in the bar. A chip searches by that exact thing, not by its name: the creator Pompeu brings what is his, and nothing that merely mentions the word.",
  "tourSearching3Title": "Chips stack",
  "tourSearching3Body": "Two chips show what meets both, not what meets either: this tag, by this creator. That is what makes them worth it. Backspace on an empty field takes the last one off.",
  "tourSearching4Title": "Free text",
  "tourSearching4Body": "If what you type is not a tag or a creator, press Enter and it becomes a free-text chip: it looks in descriptions and file names. Handy for what you never got round to tagging.",
  "tourSearching5Title": "Narrowing the results",
  "tourSearching5Body": "The filter next to the bar decides what kinds of results count and lets you keep only images, only video, or only one source. With a single chip it also lets you drop whole groups of results.",
  "tourSearching6Title": "The menu filters too",
  "tourSearching6Body": "Clicking a tag in the side menu is the same as putting its chip in the bar, so you can carry on from there: add a creator, add a word. The bar always shows what is filtering.",
  "tourSearching7Title": "Every list has its own",
  "tourSearching7Body": "The tag, creator and fernie lists each have their own filter above them. That one only narrows the list you are looking at, and it does not touch what the grid is showing.",
  "tourFernie6Title": "When the whole thing is the example",
  "tourFernie6Body": "There is a button that marks the whole frame at once, for when the item is nothing but the thing. And on a fernie card, another one takes an entire tag and marks all of its content that way.",
  "tourFernie7Title": "From a suggestion to an example",
  "tourFernie7Body": "When a model has already proposed something, the panel offers to turn what it saw into regions: it draws the boxes and you accept the ones that are right. It is the fastest way to grow a fernie.",
  "tourFernie8Title": "Marking also tags",
  "tourFernie8Body": "Marking a region on an item says that the fernie appears there, so what it links to gets put on the item straight away. The creator only if it did not have one of its own.",
  "tourFernie9Title": "And then you train",
  "tourFernie9Body": "Fernies on their own recognise nothing. What recognises is a model trained with them, and that is the next tour.",
  "tourModels9Title": "Each branch has its condition",
  "tourModels9Body": "A child only runs when its parent detects the fernie you set on that link. That is the point: a general model filters, and only what it finds goes on to the specialised ones.",
  "tourModels10Title": "When it turned out wrong",
  "tourModels10Body": "You can stop a training run halfway, and you can make a model forget what it learned: its weights go and it is untrained again, but its fernies, its settings and its place in the tree stay.",
  "tourDuplicates6Title": "And it looks on its own",
  "tourDuplicates6Body": "Every so often FeRN goes over it by itself and tells you if it found something. That period is in Settings too, and so is turning it off.",
  "tourNsfw1Title": "What the lock is",
  "tourNsfw1Body": "A password that hides part of your library. What you mark as NSFW disappears while the lock is closed: it is not in the grid, not in searches, not in the counts.",
  "tourNsfw2Title": "Setting it up",
  "tourNsfw2Body": "In Settings, under the lock section, you set a password. FeRN gives you a recovery code at that moment: write it down. It is the only way back in, because the password is not stored anywhere.",
  "tourNsfw3Title": "Opening and closing",
  "tourNsfw3Body": "Opening asks for the password; closing asks for nothing and is instant. You can have it remember that it is open between sessions, or make it start closed every time.",
  "tourNsfw4Title": "Marking a tag or a creator",
  "tourNsfw4Body": "Their cards have a lock button. Marking a tag hides everything carrying it, and everything hanging below it in the tree. Marking a creator hides them and all of their content.",
  "tourNsfw5Title": "Or one item at a time",
  "tourNsfw5Body": "Any item can be marked on its own from the viewer, whatever its tags say. And when you import you can have everything that arrives come in marked, which saves marking fifty things by hand.",
  "tourNsfw6Title": "How it behaves",
  "tourNsfw6Body": "You choose what closed means: marked content vanishing altogether, or showing up blurred so you know it is there. And you choose whether opening the lock shows everything or only what is marked.",
  "tourNsfw7Title": "Links can be marked too",
  "tourNsfw7Body": "A tag or a creator can have addresses marked as NSFW: they are not shown while the lock is closed, but they keep tagging what arrives from them. Hiding an address is not the same as forgetting it.",
  "tourNsfw8Title": "Getting rid of it all",
  "tourNsfw8Body": "In Settings, under Database, you can wipe only what is marked — content only; tags, creators, fernies and models stay. It is offered only while the lock is open: closed, you would be deleting blind.",
  "tourFiles1Title": "Where your content lives",
  "tourFiles1Body": "FeRN keeps a database of what it knows, and the files stay on your disk. In Settings you choose the library folder: that is where what you bring in goes.",
  "tourFiles2Title": "Watching a folder of your own",
  "tourFiles2Body": "You can point FeRN at a folder you already have and bring in what is inside. You decide whether it copies the files into the library or leaves them where they are and just remembers them.",
  "tourFiles3Title": "Tidying on its own",
  "tourFiles3Body": "FeRN can file what it manages into folders by source, by creator or by date, so the disk looks like the library. Change your mind later and it moves what is already there.",
  "tourFiles4Title": "The avatars folder",
  "tourFiles4Body": "Avatars are copies FeRN makes, so they live in their own folder, which you also choose. Changing the folder moves what is in it: nothing loses its picture.",
  "tourFiles5Title": "Moving everything",
  "tourFiles5Body": "If you point the library at another disk, FeRN offers to move what it manages there and update every path it has. It is one operation and it can take a while, so it tells you how it went.",
  "tourFiles6Title": "Files nobody uses",
  "tourFiles6Body": "Over time, orphans pile up: avatars you replaced, downloads whose entry you discarded, weights from a model that is gone. The cleanup button in Settings finds them, says how much they weigh, and asks before deleting.",
  "tourFiles7Title": "Starting over",
  "tourFiles7Body": "Wiping the database is the only thing here with no way back, and it asks twice. You choose whether it takes the files with it: without that, your files stay where they are and a scan brings them back.",
  "tourFiles8Title": "And it tells you when it is done",
  "tourFiles8Body": "Long jobs — importing, training, recognising, looking for repeats — can notify you when they finish, with or without a sound. That is in Settings too, and you can pick which ones.",
  "viewerVolume": "Volume",
